From: OpenBMC X570D4I-2T port <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] redfish: accept a Boot object written back as read

Clients that set a boot override by PATCHing back the whole Boot object
they read from /redfish/v1/Systems/system, as gofish's
ComputerSystem.SetBoot does for bmclib (Tinkerbell's Rufio), got 400
Bad Request, so the override never landed. bmclib then retried with
only BootSourceOverrideTarget and BootSourceOverrideEnabled, which left
BootSourceOverrideMode at whatever the BIOS last wrote (Legacy), and
the UEFI netboot did not happen. Three things in the echoed object were
refused:

- BootSourceOverrideTarget@Redfish.AllowableValues (and any other
  property annotation) failed with PropertyUnknown. The JSON reader now
  ignores keys of the form "<Property>@<Term>" unless a handler asks
  for one by name. Object-level annotations (a leading '@', such as
  @Redfish.SettingsApplyTime) change what a request means and are still
  refused unless handled; top-level @odata.* keys were already dropped.

- TrustedModuleRequiredToBoot was read as a JSON bool, while the schema
  and this service's own GET render it as the enum "Required" /
  "Disabled", so writing back what was read failed with
  PropertyValueTypeError. It is now read as the enum. The web UI sets
  the TPM policy over D-Bus REST, not this property.

- RemainingAutomaticRetryAttempts is read-only and failed with
  PropertyUnknown. It is now reported as PropertyNotWritable in
  @Message.ExtendedInfo while the rest of the request is applied; a
  request that writes nothing else still fails with PropertyNotWritable.

Everything else the GET renders under Boot (AutomaticRetryAttempts,
AutomaticRetryConfig, StopBootOnFault, the override properties) was
already writable.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/redfish-core/include/utils/json_utils.hpp
+++ b/redfish-core/include/utils/json_utils.hpp
@@ -519,6 +519,18 @@ struct PerUnpack
     bool complete = false;
 };
 
+// A property annotation ("BootSourceOverrideTarget@Redfish.AllowableValues",
+// "Members@odata.count") describes a property; it is not one, and nothing can
+// write it. Clients that PATCH back an object they read, as gofish's
+// ComputerSystem.SetBoot does with the whole Boot object, send them along.
+// Object-level annotations ("@Redfish.SettingsApplyTime") carry request
+// semantics, so a leading '@' is not one of these.
+inline bool isPropertyAnnotation(std::string_view key)
+{
+    size_t at = key.find('@');
+    return at != std::string_view::npos && at != 0;
+}
+
 inline bool readJsonHelperObject(nlohmann::json::object_t& obj,
                                  crow::Response& res,
                                  std::span<PerUnpack> toUnpack)
@@ -589,7 +601,9 @@ inline bool readJsonHelperObject(nlohmann::json::object_t& obj,
             break;
         }
 
-        if (unpackIndex == toUnpack.size())
+        // A caller that names an annotation key still gets it; any other is
+        // ignored rather than failing the whole request.
+        if (unpackIndex == toUnpack.size() && !isPropertyAnnotation(item.first))
         {
             messages::propertyUnknown(res, item.first);
             result = false;
